  "note": "Shared conformance corpus for the JSON-Schema `duration` format (2020-12 §7.3.1 -> RFC 3339 Appendix A -> ISO 8601 duration). Unlike pattern_conformance, there is ONE generator-owned PINNED regex (`pinned_regex`, below) applied to every `value`; each engine compiles that single regex and reports whether it matches. `expect` is the strict RFC-3339-ABNF verdict (true = a valid duration). A conformant run requires: (1) every engine matches the pinned regex identically to every other engine, AND (2) that shared verdict equals `expect` for every value. Key ABNF facts encoded here: fractions are FORBIDDEN; >=1 component required (bare P/PT invalid); T required before time parts; PnW mutually exclusive with the Y/M/D/H/M/S form; and the components NEST strictly (dur-year = n Y [dur-month]; dur-month = n M [dur-day]) so a HIGHER unit may only be followed by the units BELOW it in order -- e.g. `P1Y4D` (year then day, SKIPPING month) is INVALID, while `P1Y6M4D` is valid. Digit classes are written [0-9] (not \\d) in the pinned regex so even the Rust `regex` crate -- whose \\d is Unicode-aware -- agrees ASCII-only without relying on a per-engine ASCII flag.",
  "pinned_regex": "^P(?:(?:[0-9]+Y(?:[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+D)?)?|[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+D)?|[0-9]+D)(?:T(?:[0-9]+H(?:[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+S)?)?|[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+S)?|[0-9]+S))?|T(?:[0-9]+H(?:[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+S)?)?|[0-9]+M(?:[0-9]+S)?|[0-9]+S)|[0-9]+W)$",
